/**
 * @page DspCore_Numeric Numeric and buffer contracts
 *
 * This specification governs DSP components; the module topics distinguish
 * implemented APIs from planned transforms.
 *
 * @section DspCore_Numeric_Types Samples and arithmetic
 *
 * - **SPEC-NUM-1**: Floating-point processing uses float by default. Algorithms
 *   may offer explicit double arithmetic; choosing double storage must never
 *   silently reduce computation to float. Public scalar choices do not change
 *   with the target. float has binary radix, at least 24 significand bits and
 *   an exponent range including binary32; double is at least as precise and
 *   wide as float. double need not be 64 bits (notably on AVR).
 * - **SPEC-NUM-2**: Storage and arithmetic are separate contracts. Initial
 *   processing kernels use floating-point arithmetic. Signed integer PCM is
 *   a storage representation, not an implicit fixed-point implementation.
 *   Fixed-point kernels require a separate format/overflow contract and a
 *   Foundation arithmetic prerequisite; they are not implemented here.
 * - **SPEC-NUM-3**: Nominal audio samples are bipolar [-1,1]; nominal envelopes are unipolar [0,1]. Generic processing permits headroom and
 *   never clips implicitly. A component must state different units or ranges,
 *   such as hertz, seconds or cycles.
 * - **SPEC-NUM-4**: Explicit normalized signed PCM conversion maps -1 to the
 *   integer minimum and +1 to its maximum, saturates outside that interval,
 *   and rounds intermediate values to nearest with ties away from zero.
 *   Each sign uses its own full-scale magnitude. NaN maps to zero and
 *   infinities saturate. Conversion must check bounds before integer casts;
 *   signed overflow and out-of-range casts are forbidden. These are future
 *   conversion requirements, not a conversion API in this release.
 * - **SPEC-NUM-5**: Checked setup rejects non-finite parameters and invalid
 *   domains without changing existing valid state. Stateful processing must
 *   document its non-finite sample policy before implementation. Stateless
 *   arithmetic may propagate NaN/Inf; it must not cause unsafe casts or memory
 *   access. Denormal flushing is never enabled globally by the library.
 * - **SPEC-NUM-6**: Error tolerances depend on the algorithm and actual scalar
 *   precision. Tests use reference values plus absolute/relative tolerances,
 *   not a blanket exact-equality rule. Compile success proves no throughput
 *   or real-time guarantee. Hardware performance must be measured separately.
 *
 * @section DspCore_Numeric_Buffers Storage and failure
 *
 * - **SPEC-BUF-1**: Processing accepts caller-owned buffers and workspace;
 *   processing calls do not allocate. Optional owning LUT containers may
 *   allocate only through explicitly documented storage operations. Callers
 *   can reserve capacity before time-critical processing.
 * - **SPEC-BUF-2**: Counts describe elements. Every dimension multiplication
 *   and count-times-sizeof multiplication is checked before allocation or
 *   indexing. Invalid sizes and allocation failure return failure without
 *   changing dimensions, capacity, pointer or existing samples.
 * - **SPEC-BUF-3**: Empty buffers allow a null pointer and are safe no-ops;
 *   nonempty buffers require adequate non-null storage. Checked entry points
 *   reject invalid descriptors before writing. Raw-pointer APIs cannot prove
 *   allocation extent: the caller supplies the stated accessible extent.
 * - **SPEC-BUF-4**: Owning LUTs are move-only and store arithmetic sample
 *   types, excluding bool. New logical elements are zero-initialized; reserve
 *   preserves logical size and values. Resizing within capacity reuses storage.
 *   Empty resize retains capacity; explicit release clears pointer, dimensions
 *   and capacity. Moved-from objects are valid and empty. Containers owning
 *   heap memory are not trivially copyable value types.
 * - **SPEC-BUF-5**: Multidimensional data uses x-contiguous row-major storage:
 *   2D index y*width+x; 3D index (z*height+y)*width+x. Any zero extent makes
 *   the shape empty and canonicalizes all extents to zero. Resize preserves
 *   the flat prefix; preserving geometric coordinates requires an explicit
 *   reshape/copy operation. Storage never invokes a virtual generator.
 * - **SPEC-BUF-6**: Out-of-place APIs require disjoint input, output and
 *   workspace ranges unless overlap is explicitly supported. In-place APIs
 *   must be named/documented and tested for that use. No hidden scratch
 *   allocation compensates for overlap; APIs that cannot check overlap state
 *   it as a caller precondition.
 *
 * @section DspCore_Numeric_Boundaries Coordinates and boundaries
 *
 * - **SPEC-DOM-1**: Interpolation arithmetic is separate from lookup boundary
 *   handling. Lookup explicitly selects clamp, wrap or rejection; periodic
 *   phase is measured in cycles and wraps to [0,1), including negative phase.
 *   Checked coordinate interpolation rejects collapsed/non-finite bounds;
 *   singleton LUT axes are handled by lookup without dividing by zero.
 * - **SPEC-DOM-2**: Segment sample ranges are half-open [first,last). Empty
 *   ranges write nothing; singleton segments write the start value. Longer
 *   segments include both endpoints exactly (except constant singleton LUT shapes).
 *   Curve zero has a defined linear
 *   limit, evaluated stably for small magnitudes. A singleton shape LUT acts
 *   as a constant across the requested segment.
 *
 * See @ref DspCore_Architecture for scope and planned modules.
 */
